Relevant tables / columns and their possible usage  

• NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE.CITIBIKE_TRIPS  
  – "starttime", "stoptime": filter trips in calendar‑year 2018  
  – "start_station_latitude", "start_station_longitude": build GEOGRAPHY point of the start of trip  
  – "end_station_latitude", "end_station_longitude": build GEOGRAPHY point of the end of trip  
  – "usertype": group (Customer vs Subscriber)  
  – other columns let you inspect data, ids, etc.  

• NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.ZIP_CODES  
  – "zip_code": value ‘10019’ (Manhattan)  
  – "zip_code_geom": GEOGRAPHY / GEOMETRY polygon used with ST_WITHIN() to decide if a point is inside the ZIP.  

• NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.NOAA_GSOD.STATIONS  
  – "usaf", "wban", "name": find the New York ‑ Central Park weather station and pick its identifiers.  

• NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.NOAA_GSOD.GSOD2018    (similar layout for every year)  
  – "stn" (=usaf), "wban": select the Central Park rows  
  – "da","mo","year": compose calendar date that matches a trip date  
  – "temp": mean daily temperature (°F)  
  – "prcp": total precipitation (inch)  
  – "wdsp": mean wind speed (knot)  

Conditions that will be used  

1. GSOD rows → WHERE "stn" = '<Central Park usaf>' AND "wban" = '<Central Park wban>'  
2. Trips rows → WHERE YEAR(TO_DATE(TO_TIMESTAMP_NTZ("starttime"/1000000))) = 2018  
3. Start/end points within ZIP geometry:  
   ST_WITHIN(ST_MAKEPOINT("start_station_longitude","start_station_latitude"), z."zip_code_geom")  
   and the same for the end point; z."zip_code" = '10019'  

-------------------------------------------------------------------
Sample exploration and build‑up queries  
-------------------------------------------------------------------

```sql
-- 1. Description: Find weather‑station records whose name contains ‘Central Park’
SELECT 
    "usaf",
    "wban",
    "name"
FROM NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.NOAA_GSOD.STATIONS
WHERE "name" ILIKE '%central%park%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Look at 2018 GSOD rows for the candidate Central Park station
SELECT 
    "stn",
    "wban",
    "year",
    "mo",
    "da",
    "temp",
    "prcp",
    "wdsp"
FROM NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.NOAA_GSOD.GSOD2018
WHERE "stn" = '725033'   -- replace with id seen in query 1
  AND "wban" = '94728'   -- replace with id seen in query 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Compute 2018 average T, precipitation, wind at Central Park
SELECT 
    AVG("temp")  AS avg_temp_f,
    AVG("prcp")  AS avg_prcp_in,
    AVG("wdsp")::FLOAT AS avg_wdsp_kts
FROM NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.NOAA_GSOD.GSOD2018
WHERE "stn"  = '725033'
  AND "wban" = '94728';
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Inspect raw trip timestamps to verify 2018 values
SELECT 
    "starttime",
    "stoptime",
    TO_DATE(TO_TIMESTAMP_NTZ("starttime"/1000000)) AS trip_date
FROM NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE.CITIBIKE_TRIPS
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Fetch the geometry for Manhattan ZIP 10019
SELECT 
    "zip_code",
    "zip_code_geom"
FROM NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.ZIP_CODES
WHERE "zip_code" = '10019';
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Test if sample start points fall inside the 10019 polygon
SELECT 
    t."start_station_latitude",
    t."start_station_longitude",
    ST_WITHIN(
        ST_MAKEPOINT(t."start_station_longitude", t."start_station_latitude"),
        z."zip_code_geom"
    ) AS start_in_10019
FROM NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE.CITIBIKE_TRIPS t
CROSS JOIN NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.ZIP_CODES z
WHERE z."zip_code" = '10019'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Pull trips whose start AND end are inside ZIP 10019 (calendar 2018)
SELECT 
    t."usertype",
    TO_DATE(TO_TIMESTAMP_NTZ(t."starttime"/1000000)) AS trip_date
FROM NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE.CITIBIKE_TRIPS t
CROSS JOIN NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.ZIP_CODES z
WHERE z."zip_code" = '10019'
  AND ST_WITHIN(
        ST_MAKEPOINT(t."start_station_longitude", t."start_station_latitude"),
        z."zip_code_geom")
  AND ST_WITHIN(
        ST_MAKEPOINT(t."end_station_longitude", t."end_station_latitude"),
        z."zip_code_geom")
  AND YEAR(TO_DATE(TO_TIMESTAMP_NTZ(t."starttime"/1000000))) = 2018
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Join trips (inside 10019, 2018) with Central Park weather by trip date
SELECT 
    t."usertype",
    w."temp",
    w."prcp",
    w."wdsp"
FROM NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE.CITIBIKE_TRIPS t
CROSS JOIN NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.ZIP_CODES z
JOIN NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.NOAA_GSOD.GSOD2018 w
  ON w."stn"  = '725033'
 AND w."wban" = '94728'
 AND TO_DATE(TO_TIMESTAMP_NTZ(t."starttime"/1000000)) 
     = TO_DATE(TO_DATE(w."year"||'-'||w."mo"||'-'||w."da"))
WHERE z."zip_code" = '10019'
  AND ST_WITHIN(
        ST_MAKEPOINT(t."start_station_longitude", t."start_station_latitude"),
        z."zip_code_geom")
  AND ST_WITHIN(
        ST_MAKEPOINT(t."end_station_longitude", t."end_station_latitude"),
        z."zip_code_geom")
  AND YEAR(TO_DATE(TO_TIMESTAMP_NTZ(t."starttime"/1000000))) = 2018
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Per usertype, compute average weather values attached to its trips
SELECT 
    t."usertype",
    AVG(w."temp")                      AS avg_temp_f,
    AVG(w."prcp")                      AS avg_prcp_in,
    AVG(w."wdsp")::FLOAT               AS avg_wdsp_kts
FROM NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE.CITIBIKE_TRIPS t
CROSS JOIN NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.ZIP_CODES z
JOIN NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.NOAA_GSOD.GSOD2018 w
  ON w."stn"  = '725033'
 AND w."wban" = '94728'
 AND TO_DATE(TO_TIMESTAMP_NTZ(t."starttime"/1000000)) 
     = TO_DATE(TO_DATE(w."year"||'-'||w."mo"||'-'||w."da"))
WHERE z."zip_code" = '10019'
  AND ST_WITHIN(
        ST_MAKEPOINT(t."start_station_longitude", t."start_station_latitude"),
        z."zip_code_geom")
  AND ST_WITHIN(
        ST_MAKEPOINT(t."end_station_longitude", t."end_station_latitude"),
        z."zip_code_geom")
  AND YEAR(TO_DATE(TO_TIMESTAMP_NTZ(t."starttime"/1000000))) = 2018
GROUP BY t."usertype"
ORDER BY avg_temp_f DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Final query – return the usertype with the highest average temperature
SELECT 
    first_value("usertype")     OVER (ORDER BY avg_temp_f DESC) AS hottest_usertype,
    first_value(avg_temp_f)     OVER (ORDER BY avg_temp_f DESC) AS avg_temp_f,
    first_value(avg_prcp_in)    OVER (ORDER BY avg_temp_f DESC) AS avg_prcp_in,
    first_value(avg_wdsp_kts)   OVER (ORDER BY avg_temp_f DESC) AS avg_wdsp_kts
FROM (
    SELECT 
        t."usertype",
        AVG(w."temp")  AS avg_temp_f,
        AVG(w."prcp")  AS avg_prcp_in,
        AVG(w."wdsp")::FLOAT AS avg_wdsp_kts
    FROM NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE.CITIBIKE_TRIPS t
    CROSS JOIN NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.ZIP_CODES z
    JOIN NEW_YORK_CITIBIKE_1.NOAA_GSOD.GSOD2018 w
      ON w."stn"  = '725033'
     AND w."wban" = '94728'
     AND TO_DATE(TO_TIMESTAMP_NTZ(t."starttime"/1000000)) 
         = TO_DATE(TO_DATE(w."year"||'-'||w."mo"||'-'||w."da"))
    WHERE z."zip_code" = '10019'
      AND ST_WITHIN(
            ST_MAKEPOINT(t."start_station_longitude", t."start_station_latitude"),
            z."zip_code_geom")
      AND ST_WITHIN(
            ST_MAKEPOINT(t."end_station_longitude", t."end_station_latitude"),
            z."zip_code_geom")
      AND YEAR(TO_DATE(TO_TIMESTAMP_NTZ(t."starttime"/1000000))) = 2018
    GROUP BY t."usertype"
)
LIMIT 1;
```

Each query returns up to 20 rows (or 1 in the last step) and is enclosed in proper Snowflake double‑quotes for column names. Replace the station identifiers in queries 2‑10 with the actual "usaf"/"wban" you find in query 1.
